 ## Overview
-The Fenwick Tree (also known as the Half-tree or Partial Sum Tree) is a compact data structure that stores partial sums to enable $O(\log n)$ point updates and prefix-sum queries [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently maintaining cumulative sums in dynamic environments, such as the incremental integration of light transport in volume rendering [S1]. Unlike a standard complete binary tree which requires $2n$ nodes for $n$ leaves, the Fenwick Tree variant exploits the fact that prefix-sum queries starting from index $0$ never require the right child of any node, storing only $1.5n$ elements [S1]. This reduces memory usage and the number of nodes modified during updates by half compared to the full tree representation [S1]. It builds on [Group Prefix Sum for Shell Lists](2746-group-prefix-sum-for-shell-lists.md).
